                <h2>Background: Three Efficiency Killers in Agency Account Opening</h2>
<p>Operations staff in overseas ad agency account opening often face this dilemma: once client volume increases, account status becomes a mess—sales says materials have been sent, business development says initial review is complete, and media buyers say the account hasn't arrived yet. Where's the problem?</p>
<p><strong>Information silos</strong> are the core issue. Agency account opening involves multiple stages: sales对接, material collection, initial review submission, platform review, and account activation. Each stage has different responsible parties, and status updates rely entirely on verbal or group chat communication. Once any node delays, the entire chain's delivery time extends.</p>
<p>Kanban management solves this problem. It visualizes the account opening process, makes each account's status clear at a glance, automatically triggers alerts for exceptions, and shifts team collaboration from "people chasing tasks" to "tasks chasing people."</p>
<h2>Core Module Design for Account Opening Kanban</h2>
<p>A practical account opening Kanban should include at least four columns:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Column</th>
<th>Trigger Condition</th>
<th>Responsible Party</th>
<th>Timeout Alert</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Pending Materials</td>
<td>Client confirms account opening intent</td>
<td>Sales</td>
<td>Over 24 hours without supplement</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Under Review</td>
<td>Materials submitted to platform</td>
<td>Business Dev</td>
<td>80% of platform's promised timeline</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Activation Pending</td>
<td>Account number received</td>
<td>Media Buyer</td>
<td>Over 48 hours without activation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Delivered</td>
<td>Account formally transferred</td>
<td>Operations</td>
<td>None</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Each card should record at minimum: client name, platform type (Meta/Google/TikTok, etc.), submission date, follow-up notes, and most recent operator.</p>
<h2>SOP Execution Steps</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Create New Account Opening Task</h3>
<p>When sales confirms account opening intent with the client, immediately create a card in the Kanban, filling in client name, target platform, and ad budget range. Set "Pending Materials" as the default column.</p>
<p><strong>Key Operation</strong>: Card creation must occur within 2 hours of client confirming intent to avoid omissions.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Material Completeness Check</h3>
<p>Operations staff verify materials against platform requirements. Common omissions include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Business license not translated or stamp unclear</li>
<li>Website domain inconsistent with license entity</li>
<li>Landing page missing privacy policy or refund policy</li>
<li>Industry-specific qualifications missing (finance, gaming, etc.)</li>
</ul>
<p>When gaps are found, mark "Missing XX material" in card notes and @ relevant personnel.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Submission and Status Tracking</h3>
<p>After materials are complete, move to "Under Review" column. Record submission time and set reminders based on platform's common review cycles:</p>
<ul>
<li>Meta Business Manager: Typically 3-5 business days</li>
<li>Google Ads: Typically 1-3 business days</li>
<li>TikTok for Business: Typically 2-4 business days</li>
</ul>
<p>If approaching timeout threshold, the card automatically turns red, and operations should proactively contact platform agents or submit support tickets.</p>
<h3>Step 4: Account Activation and Delivery</h3>
<p>After receiving account approval notification, move card to "Activation Pending." Media buyers complete the following:</p>
<ol>
<li>Verify account login status</li>
<li>Confirm account limits and recharge information</li>
<li>Complete initial top-up test</li>
<li>Record account ID, password storage location, and recharge link on the card</li>
</ol>
<p>After confirmation, drag card to "Delivered" and notify client of account information.</p>
<h2>Common Problem Handling Checklist</h2>
<p><strong>Q: What if client materials need repeated revisions, causing progress to regress?</strong></p>
<p>Don't delete cards in the Kanban; instead, record revision versions (V1/V2). After each revision, add timestamp notes explaining the reason for this change for later review.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How to handle platform review delays?</strong></p>
<p>First check if secondary review was triggered (high-risk industries, high-budget accounts). After confirmation, record ticket number and estimated response time on the card, and synchronize updates with the client to avoid churn due to unknown status.</p>
<p><strong>Q: When multiple clients are opening accounts simultaneously, how to prioritize?</strong></p>
<p>Add "Priority" tags (High/Medium/Low) to cards, judging based on client budget volume and cooperation stage. High-priority cards should be pinned to the top with dedicated personnel assigned.</p>
<h2>Conclusion</h2>
<p>The essence of Kanban management is making information flow transparent and traceable. For agency account opening services, every "stuck" node may be a precursor to client churn. Through standardized Kanban processes, operations teams can not only improve delivery efficiency but also demonstrate professionalism in client communication, enhancing renewal willingness.</p>
<p>For teams initially adopting Kanban, it's recommended to use tools like Notion, Trello, or Feishu Tasks, prioritize running through one complete process, then gradually expand to more business modules like recharge management and ad delivery.</p>
